# Native Workflow segment scripts MUST be checked out with LF endings.
#
# Why this is load-bearing, not cosmetic: Claude Code's permission layer rejects a
# `Workflow` invocation whose `scriptPath` file contains carriage returns —
# "script contains control characters that would be hidden in the approval dialog".
# On a Windows checkout with core.autocrlf=true these files arrive CRLF, so every
# workflow-path skill (harness / spec / debug / deep-review / codebase-audit /
# migrate / refactor / test-gen) fails to launch. Reproduced 2026-08-03: the same
# script rejected as CRLF launched successfully as a byte-identical LF copy.
#
# Limits, stated honestly:
#   - Effective for NEW clones/checkouts only. The index blobs are already LF
#     (`git ls-files --eol` -> `i/lf w/crlf`), so `git add --renormalize` is a
#     no-op and produces no diff.
#   - An ALREADY-INSTALLED plugin cache is not fixed retroactively; that copy
#     needs a re-clone or a plugin reinstall.
#   - To fix an existing worktree in place: remove the files and re-check them out
#     (`rm workflows/*.workflow.js && git checkout -- workflows/`).
*.workflow.js text eol=lf

# Shell scripts and workflow YAML must be LF too. Two failure modes were measured on
# 2026-08-21 in a CR-intolerant toolchain (Linux-native / WSL: GNU grep 3.11, bash
# 5.2.21); both are NON-reproducible under Git Bash (GNU grep 3.0, bash 5.2.37-msys),
# which swallows a trailing CR. That tolerance is the exception, not the rule, and the
# CI runner is ubuntu-latest.
#   - A line-oriented or `$`-anchored regex over a CRLF file stops matching: `grep -c`
#     returns 0 where the byte-identical LF copy returns 1.
#   - A CRLF shell script does not run at all. The first line `set -euo pipefail` picks
#     up the CR as part of the option name -> `set: pipefail: invalid option name`,
#     exit 2.
#
# Scope, as precisely as the block above: `text eol=lf` fixes the index on check-in and
# the working tree on checkout. It does NOT rewrite a file already sitting in the
# working tree, so a file this or any other session writes with CRLF still needs fixing
# by hand -- `git ls-files --eol` is the detector.
# The three existing `.github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/*.yml` are already `i/lf` (measured), so
# these lines produce no index diff for them.
*.sh  text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
